localized.description:
Tallis' motet "O sacrum convivium" for 5 voices was one of 17 sacred works that he dedicated to Queen Elizabeth I on the occasion of her 17th year on the throne of England.  Despite his life-long allegiance to the Catholic faith, his Queen, Elizabeth, an Anglican Protestant, accepted his preference for traditional Latin text and intricate imitative polyphony. This work was intended as an antiphon to the Magnificat at Second Vespers of Corpus Christi. It is a short segment of the prayer honoring the Blessed Sacrament.

The soundtrack for this video was created from a MIDI file created by Robert Urmann and uploaded to CPDL.org . I entered the text in Latin using a Spanish language voice bank (MAIKA) and the Vocaloid 4 voice encoder program. In this latest rendition, I corrected some vowel sounds, created a more legato effect, added some natural dynamics, adjusted the reverb / echo and rebalanced the voices. I also applied an "English" (rather than "Italian") pronunciation scheme for the Latin text as Tallis probably would have known (e.g. "ejus" is "eʒus" instead of "eius"; "gratia" is "grasja" instead of "grat͡sja"; and "pignus" is "pignus" instead of "pinjus.").

Text:

O sacrum convivium, in quo Christus sumitur;
recolitur memoria passionis ejus;
mens impletur gratia;
et futurae gloriae nobis pignus datur.

Translation:

O sacred banquet, in which Christ is received;
the memory of his passion is recollected;
the mind is full of grace;
and future glory shall be given to us.

Description of the photos:
1. Descant part from "O sacrum convivium," Thomas Tallis, Cantiones quae ab argumento sacrae vocantur, 1575
2. Dispute of the Sacrament, Raffaello Sanzio, 1510
3. Chapel Royal, Hampton Court Palace, the main venue of Tallis where he entertained royal audiences
4. Detail of the monstrance, Dispute of the Sacrament, Raffaello Sanzio, 1510
5. Chapel Royal, Hampton Court Palace
6. Silver communion cup, John Jones, 1572
7. Portrait of Queen Elizabeth I with royal retinue, c1569
